      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out whether the statement \"The tiger chases the lion\" is true, false, or unknown based on the given information. Let me start by carefully reading the context and the rules provided.\n\nFirst, I'll list out all the information given:\n\n1. The dog chases the squirrel.\n2. The dog likes the tiger.\n3. The lion chases the squirrel.\n4. The lion is big.\n5. The lion is red.\n6. The lion likes the tiger.\n7. The lion visits the squirrel.\n8. The lion visits the tiger.\n9. The squirrel chases the lion.\n10. The squirrel is nice.\n11. The squirrel likes the lion.\n12. The tiger chases the dog.\n13. The tiger is kind.\n14. The tiger is red.\n15. The tiger likes the dog.\n16. The tiger likes the lion.\n17. If something chases the lion then the lion is blue.\n18. If something is kind then it chases the tiger.\n19. If something chases the tiger then it visits the dog.\n20. If something visits the lion then it likes the squirrel.\n21. If something is red and it chases the tiger then it likes the squirrel.\n22. If something chases the tiger and it visits the squirrel then the squirrel visits the tiger.\n23. If something visits the dog then the dog likes the lion.\n24. If something is kind and it visits the dog then the dog is kind.\n\nNow, the question is whether the tiger chases the lion. Let's break this down.\n\nFrom the context, I know that the lion chases the squirrel (point 3). Also, the squirrel chases the lion (point 9). So, the lion is chasing the squirrel, and the squirrel is chasing the lion. That means they are mutual chasers.\n\nLooking at the rules provided, let's see if any of them directly address whether the tiger chases the lion. Rule 18 says if something is kind, it chases the tiger. But the tiger is kind (point 13), so it chases the tiger. However, that doesn't necessarily mean it chases the lion.\n\nRule 19 says if something chases the tiger, then it visits the dog. The tiger chases the dog (point 12), so 