t 14 says young things are white, so if she's white and young, she's cold. But point 16 says if she's furry and white, she's smart, which would make her not white, hence not cold. This seems like a contradiction.\n\nWait, maybe I'm overcomplicating it. Let's try to map out the relationships.\n\nFrom point 16: If something is both furry and white, then it's smart. So, if Erin is furry and white, she's smart. But from point 15: If something is nice and white, it's smart. So, if Erin is nice and white, she's smart. But from point 14: All young, white things are cold. So, if Erin is white and young, she's cold. But from point 13: All young things are white, so if she's young, she's white. So, if she's white and young, she's cold. But from point 16, if she's furry and white, she's smart. So, if she's smart, she's not white, hence not cold. But that's a contradiction because if she's smart, she's not white, so she can't be cold. Therefore, Erin can't be both smart and white, which would mean she's not cold. So, the statement \"Erin is cold\" is false because Erin is smart, not white, and thus not cold.\n\nWait, but let me check if there's another way. Maybe Erin is not white. But point 4 says she's white. So, she is white. Therefore, from point 14, she's young. From point 9, she's smart because she's nice and smart. So, she's smart, not white, hence not cold. Therefore, the statement \"Erin is cold\" is false.\n</think>\n\nThe statement \"Erin is cold\" is false because Erin is smart, not white, and thus not cold.\n\n**Answer: B) False**",
