1 says blue, smart things are furry, so if she's smart, she's furry.\n\nWait, but point 13 says if someone is smart and young, they are not furry. So if Erin is smart and young, she can't be furry. But from point 11, if she's blue and smart, she's furry. So this creates a contradiction unless she's neither smart nor young.\n\nWait, but point 9 says if something is young and blue, then it's smart. So if Erin is young and blue, she's smart, which would make her furry (from point 11). But point 13 says if she's smart and young, she's not furry. So that's a problem because she can't be both smart and young if she's furry and not furry at the same time.\n\nSo maybe Erin isn't smart or young. Let's see. From point 16, if someone is kind, they're furry. But Erin isn't kind, so that doesn't help. From point 14, if someone is not furry, they aren't smart. So if Erin is not furry, then she's not smart. But from point 9, if she's young and blue, she's smart. So if she's not smart, she's not young and blue. So she's either not young or not blue.\n\nBut she is blue, so she's not young. So she's not smart because if she were smart, she would have to be young and blue, which she isn't. So she's not smart. Now, from point 11, blue, smart things are furry. Since she's not smart, she's not furry. From point 12, young things are nice. She's not young, so she's not nice. Therefore, Erin is not nice.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. If she's not smart, then from point 11, she's not furry. From point 12, she's not nice. So yes, Erin is not nice. Therefore, the statement \"Erin is nice\" is false.\n</think>\n\nThe statement \"Erin is nice\" is false.\n\n**Answer: B) False**",
