m point 9, if Gary is cold, then he's smart. So if Gary is cold, he's smart, and thus furry. But does that make Gary big? Not necessarily. Because point 10 says big, blue things are round, but it doesn't say anything about being big. So Gary could be big and blue, making him round and thus cold, but that doesn't make him big. Wait, but point 10 says big, blue things are round, so if Gary is big and blue, then it's round, making him cold. But does that mean Gary is big? Or is there another way to see that?\n\nWait, maybe I'm missing something. Let me try to map out the relationships:\n\n1. Gary is smart (point 6).\n2. All smart things are furry (point 7), so Gary is furry.\n3. If Gary is furry, then Gary is blue (point 11), so Gary is blue.\n4. If Gary is blue, then from point 12, round things are cold. So if Gary is round, then he's cold. But from point 9, if Gary is cold, then he's smart. So if Gary is cold, he's smart, and thus furry, which we already have. But does that make Gary big? Not necessarily. Because point 10 says big, blue things are round, but it doesn't say anything about being big. So Gary could be big and blue, making him round and thus cold, but that doesn't make him big. Wait, but point 10 says big, blue things are round, so if Gary is big and blue, then it's round, making him cold. But does that mean Gary is big? Or is there another way to see that?\n\nWait, maybe I'm getting confused. Let me try to see if Gary can be big. From point 10, big, blue things are round. So if Gary is big and blue, then it's round, making him cold. But does that mean Gary is big? Or is there another way to see that?\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can use point 12: round things are cold. So if Gary is round, then he's cold. But from point 9, if Gary is cold, then he's smart. So if Gary is cold, he's smart, and thus furry. But does that make Gary big? Not necessarily. Because point 10 says big, blue things are round, but it doesn't say anything about being big. So Gary could