otherwise.\n\nSo, applying rule 10: If Anne is kind and Anne is cold, then Anne is smart. But Bob is kind and cold, so by the same rule, Bob should be smart.\n\nAlternatively, looking at rule 12: Rough people are big. So, if someone is rough, they are big. From rule 11: Young people are red. So, if someone is young, they are red. But Bob is not necessarily young, but he is cold and kind.\n\nWait, but the rule about Anne being kind and cold leading to Anne being smart applies to Anne, not Bob. So, Bob's situation is different. Bob is kind and cold, but the rule doesn't say anything about Bob being kind and cold leading to him being smart. It only applies to Anne.\n\nSo, based on the given rules, Bob is kind and cold, so according to rule 10, he should be smart. Therefore, the statement \"Bob is smart\" should be True.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. The rules are:\n\n- Anne is kind and Anne is cold \u2192 Anne is smart.\n- Rough people are big.\n- Big people are young.\n- Red people are smart.\n- If Anne is kind and Anne is cold, then Anne is smart.\n- Young, cold people are red.\n- Rough people are big.\n- All kind people are rough.\n- If Anne is kind and Anne is cold, then Anne is smart.\n- All young people are kind.\n\nSo, the only rule that applies to Bob is rule 10, which says if someone is kind and cold, they are smart. Since Bob is kind and cold, he should be smart.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be True.\n</think>\n\nThe statement \"Bob is smart\" is True based on the given rules.\n\n**Answer: A) True**",
