e is white. That seems straightforward.\n\nBut let me check if there are any other clues that might contradict this. For example, point 10 says all quiet, kind things are white. But Bob is quiet and smart, so from point 9, he's white. So that's consistent.\n\nIs there any other information that might affect this? Let's see.\n\nPoint 15 says all white, smart things are furry. But Bob is smart, so if he were white, he would be furry. But the question is about his color, not his fur color. So that doesn't directly affect Bob's color.\n\nPoint 11 is about Erin, but she's blue, which is a different color, so it doesn't affect Bob.\n\nPoint 12 says all kind things are quiet. So if Bob is kind, he would be quiet. But Bob is smart, so from point 9, he's white. So that's consistent.\n\nPoint 14 says green things are kind. Since Harry is green, he's kind. But Bob is smart, so from point 9, he's white. So that's consistent too.\n\nPoint 8 says white, green things are smart. So since Charlie is white and kind, he's smart, which matches point 4.\n\nPoint 13 says white things are quiet. So if Bob is white, he's quiet, which matches point 1.\n\nPoint 10 says quiet, kind things are white. So since Bob is quiet and smart, he's white, which is consistent.\n\nSo all the given information points to Bob being white. Therefore, the statement \"Bob is white\" is true.\n</think>\n\nThe statement \"Bob is white\" is true based on the given information.\n\n**Answer: A) True**",
