og, then the dog visits the rabbit. Since the mouse likes the dog, the dog would visit the rabbit. But does that mean the rabbit visits the mouse? Point 14 says if someone visits the rabbit, then the rabbit visits the mouse. So, if the dog visits the rabbit, then the rabbit visits the mouse. But we don't know if the dog visits the rabbit.\n\nWait, point 15 says if someone likes the dog, then the dog visits the rabbit. The mouse likes the dog, so the dog would visit the rabbit. But does that mean the rabbit visits the mouse? Yes, because if someone visits the rabbit, then the rabbit visits the mouse. So, if the dog visits the rabbit, then the rabbit visits the mouse.\n\nBut does that mean the mouse visits the dog? Not necessarily. The rabbit visiting the mouse doesn't imply the mouse visits the dog. So, the mouse might not visit the dog.\n\nWait, but point 16 says if the mouse visits the lion and the mouse needs the dog, then the dog needs the lion. But we don't know if the mouse visits the lion.\n\nAlternatively, let's think about the statements:\n\n- The mouse likes the dog (point 7).\n- The mouse does not need the rabbit (point 8).\n- The rabbit is kind (point 9).\n- The rabbit visits the dog (point 10).\n- If someone needs the dog, they visit the lion (point 5).\n- If someone needs the mouse, they are kind (point 12).\n- If someone is round and likes the rabbit, then the rabbit visits the mouse (point 13).\n- If someone visits the rabbit, then the rabbit visits the mouse (point 14).\n- If someone likes the dog, then the dog visits the rabbit (point 15).\n- If the mouse visits the lion and the mouse needs the dog, then the dog needs the lion (point 16).\n- If the rabbit needs the lion, then the lion needs the mouse (point 17).\n- If someone visits the mouse, then the mouse needs the dog (point 18).\n\nSo, let's try to see if the mouse needs the dog.\n\nFrom point 18: If someone visits the mouse, then the mouse needs the dog. But we don't know if the mouse visits the rabbit. Wait, point 10 says the rabbit visits the dog, but that doesn't say anything about the mouse.\n\nWait, point 10 says the rabbit visits the dog, but that's about the rabbit, not the mouse. So, the mouse might not visit the rabbit.\n\nWait, point 18 says if someone visits the mouse, then the mouse needs the dog. But we don't know if the mouse visits the rabbit. So, maybe the mouse doesn't visit the rabbit, so we can't conclude anything about the mouse needing the dog.\n\nAlternatively, point 15 says if someone likes the dog, then the dog visits the rabbit. The mouse likes the dog, so the dog would visit the rabbit. But does that mean the rabbit visits the mouse? Yes, because if someone visits the rabbit, then the rabbit visits the mouse. So, if the dog visits the rabbit, then the rabbit visits the mouse.\n\nBut does that mean the mouse visits the dog? Not necessarily. The rabbit visiting the mouse doesn't imply the mouse visits the dog. So, the mouse might not visit the dog.\n\nWait, but point 16 says if the mouse visits the lion and the mouse needs the dog, then the dog needs the lion. But we don't know if the mouse visits the lion.\n\nAlternatively, let's think about the statements again.\n\nWe know the mouse likes the dog (point 7). So, does that mean the mouse needs the dog? Not necessarily. It could just like it without needing it.\n\nBut point 15 says if someone likes the dog, then the dog visits the rabbit. So, since the mouse likes the dog, the dog would visit the rabbit. But does that mean the rabbit visits the mouse? Yes, because if someone visits the rabbit, then the rabbit visits the mouse. So, if the dog visits the rabbit, then the rabbit visits the mouse.\n\nBut does that mean the mouse visits the dog? Not necessarily. The rabbit visiting the mouse doesn't imply the mouse visits the dog. So, the mouse might not visit the dog.\n\nWait, but point 18 says if someone visits the mouse, then the mouse needs the dog. But we don't know if the mouse vi