irrel (point 6). So, the lion is someone who likes the squirrel and needs it. Therefore, the squirrel must see the cow (point 7). So, the squirrel does see the cow, making the statement false.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I'm missing something. Let me check other points. The cow likes the bald eagle (point 2), sees the squirrel (point 2), and likes the lion (point 5). The lion likes the bald eagle (point 3), needs the squirrel (point 6), and sees the squirrel (point 4). The squirrel likes the lion (point 5) and, if someone likes the squirrel and needs it, then the squirrel sees the cow (point 7). So, yes, the squirrel sees the cow.\n\nTherefore, the statement is false.\n</think>\n\nThe statement \"The squirrel does not see the cow\" is false because the squirrel does see the cow.\n\n**Answer: B) False**",
